      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\n2. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\n3. Find the ratio of the two volumes.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the volume of the first cube\nThe volume \\( V_1 \\) of a cube with edge length \\( s_1 \\) is given by:\n\\[ V_1 = s_1^3 \\]\n\nFor the first cube with an edge length of six inches:\n\\[ s_1 = 6 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\\[ V_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the volume of the second cube\nThe volume \\( V_2 \\) of a cube with edge length \\( s_2 \\) is given by:\n\\[ V_2 = s_2^3 \\]\n\nFor the second cube with an edge length of one foot:\n\\[ s_2 = 1 \\text{ foot} \\]\nSince one foot is equal to 12 inches, we convert the edge length to inches:\n\\[ s_2 = 12 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\\[ V_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the ratio of the volumes\nThe ratio of the volume of the first cube to the volume of the second cube is:\n\\[ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728} \\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216.\n\n\\[ \\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8} \\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}} \\]",
