      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\n2. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\n3. Determine the ratio of these volumes.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches. The volume \\( V \\) of a cube is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is the edge length of the cube. For a cube with an edge length of six inches:\n\\[\nV_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot. There are 12 inches in a foot, so the edge length in inches of the larger cube is:\n\\[\n1 \\text{ foot} = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe volume \\( V \\) of this larger cube is:\n\\[\nV_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the volume of the smaller cube to the volume of the larger cube:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\\[\n\\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
